2 CONT THE SCOTS IN SWEDEN. 17
2 CONT
2 CONT and talented family, which, though settled in Holstein,
2 CONT originally hailed from the North of Scotland, and con-
2 CONT tributed many a famous name to art and literature. 1
2 CONT
2 CONT In the meantime the unfortunate risings of the Stuarts
2 CONT in 1716 and 1745 became the cause of a new influx of
2 CONT Scotsmen into Sweden. A curious proof of this is given
2 CONT us in a lawsuit which, in 1716, was carried on by Lars
2 CONT Gathenhielm against the captain (skeppare) of a French
2 CONT vessel called La Paix de Calais. It had been ordered
2 CONT to Scotland in order to embark and save several officers
2 CONT who had served under the Pretender, and had on board
2 CONT twenty Scotsmen, among them Lord DuiFus, who landed
2 CONT in Sweden. The vessel was to return and take up other
2 CONT fugitives in Scotland. A fate similar to that of these
2 CONT officers overtook the founder of a large business concern
2 CONT in Goteborg, which is flourishing to this day under the title
2 CONT of Carnegie & Co., viz. George Carnegie. Like so many
2 CONT others of the Pretender's adherents, he had to fly after
2 CONT the Battle of Culloden. He is said to have evaded his
2 CONT pursuers by gaining the coast and rowing out in a frail
2 CONT boat until he met with a sailing-vessel whose destination
2 CONT was Goteborg. 2 Here he soon succeeded in building up
2 CONT one of the foremost business firms. 3 After a strenuous
2 CONT activity of twenty years, Carnegie returned to Scotland,
2 CONT leaving the Goteborg business to his trusty friend and
2 CONT book-keeper, Thomas Erskine, who in 1 803 handed over the
2 CONT management of it to George Carnegie's son David. As a
2 CONT proof of the strong position of the firm and of the
2 CONT
