2 CONT
2 CONT absolute confidence placed in its management, it may here
2 CONT be mentioned that when, in the early years of the XIX th
2 CONT century, Swedish commerce had to pass through a severe
2 CONT crisis, Mr Carnegie succeeded in obtaining a loan of
2 CONT 20,000 from the Earl of Kelly, 1 at a time when the
2 CONT town itself could only raise a loan of 10,000 Riksdaler
2 CONT by giving as security the building of the East India
2 CONT Company. The great Carnegie breweries now give em-
2 CONT ployment to about one thousand workmen, for whose
2 CONT welfare in the way of housing and old-age pensions every
2 CONT thing has be
2 CONT Some time later than Carnegie, but still in the XVIIlth
2 CONT century, William Gibson from Arbroath came to Goteborg
2 CONT (in 1797), and founded another large business. He com-
2 CONT menced with a sail-cloth spinning-mill, but having been
2 CONT joined by another clever Scot, Alexander Keiller, 2 an
2 CONT engineer, he opened mechanical works and iron-foundries
2 CONT at Jonsered, which soon grew in importance. Like
2 CONT Carnegie, he showed the liveliest interest in public under-
2 CONT takings and filled many a post of honour, such as Director
2 CONT of the Savings Bank. He died in 1857, seventy-four
2 CONT years old. The business was converted into a joint-
2 CONT stock company.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Carnegie Family
2 CONT Many notable names such as a merchant named Hans Carnegie who had been active in Gothenburg as early as 1620 but he apparently died without issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT George Carnegie (-1799)
2 CONT
2 CONT After the Battle of Culloden in 1745, one George Carnegie , who in the battle had fought against his elder brother, fled to the hills of Flenesk and Glenmark with James Carnegie, Laird of Balnamoon and an Ochterlony of Guynd . The
2 CONC  three rebels briefly escaped in an open boat from Montrose, were picked up by a Swedish ship, and landed safely in Gothenburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT George had apparently studied commerce in Scotland, but broke off his education to fight for Bonnie Prince Charles, of whose lifeguard he was a member. He was outlawed after Culloden, and in Gothenburg worked first as a shop assis
2 CONC tant, but later became a wholesale dealer. In 1764 he had working in his office Thomas Erskine (later Earl of Kellie), John Hall (of British origin, although probably not a Scot, and later a man of great wealth) and James Carnegie
2 CONC  Arbuthnott the younger. The firm exported bar-iron and boards to England, and imported from England wheat, butter and pitcoal, and from Russia rye and flax. In all probability George Carnegie was also a shipowner.
2 CONT
2 CONT George employed in Gothenburg Thomas Erskine (later Earl of Kellie), John Hall and James Carnegie Arbuthnott the younger . In 1769 he returned to Scotland, where he had some years before bought back his family seat of Pittarrow, a
2 CONC nd another estate, Charleton, both in Southesk and near Montrose.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1769 he returned to Scotland, where he had some years before bought back his family seat of Pittarrow, and another estate, Charleton, both in Southesk and near Montrose. He married in the same year a Scottish woman by whom he h
2 CONC ad three sons, John, James and David. David, and James' son of the same name, maintained contact with Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT James Carnegie, "the rebel laird"
2 CONT
2 CONT James Carnegie, "the rebel laird", was third cousin to George. It is uncertain how long he remained in Sweden. He was proscribed until 1758.
2 CONT James Carnegie Arbuthnott (1740-1810)
2 CONT
2 CONT James Carnegie Arbuthnott, born in 1740, obtained his citizenship in 1766 as an inn-keeper, and was known in Gothenburg as "Old Man Carnegie of Klippan".
2 CONT
2 CONT James Carnegie Arbuthnott, son of the "the rebel laird", was born in 1740, and we first learn of him in Gothenburg in 1764, when he worked in George Carnegie's office. In 1766 he obtained his citizenship, as an inn-keeper, and was
2 CONC  known in Gothenburg as "Old Man Carnegie of Klippan". He died unmarried in 1810.
2 CONT
2 CONT David Carnegie (the Elder) (1772-1873)
2 CONT
2 CONT David Carnegie, the Elder, was born in Montrose, and was in 1786 sent to Gothenburg where from 1787-92 he worked as clerk for Thomas Erskine , the British Consul. From 1793-c. 1800 he was accountant with John Hall & Co. In 1800 Th
2 CONC omas Erskine having inherited the title Earl of Kellie returned to Scotland, and David Carnegie took over his firm. In 1799 he became a member of the Royal Bachelor's Club . He was made a burgher, as a whole-sale merchant, in 1801
2 CONC . When in 1803 he took an accountant named Jan Lamberg into partnership, the name of the firm was changed to D. Carnegie & Co.
2 CONT
2 CONT D. Carnegie & Co. exported timber and iron. From England colonial goods, wines, porter, meat, flour and salt were imported and from Russia flax and tow. The firm was also interested in herring fisheries and owned curing houses. It
2 CONC  also had certain mills in Värmland after John Hall & Co. had gone bankrupt.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1873 David Carnegie the Elder died in Gothenburg. He had a Swedish wife and a daughter, Susan Mary Ann Carnegie, from 1845 married to her cousin,
2 CONT
2 CONT David Carnegie ty (1813-1890)
2 CONT
2 CONT David Carnegie ty (1813-1890), was born in Scotland. He came to Gothenburg straight from Eton in 1830, to work in his uncle's firm. In 1836 he bought at an auction the Lorent sugar mill and brewery. Later in the same year he also 
2 CONC took over D. Carnegie & Co. The sugar mill was managed by John Nonnen and the firm of F. Malm, who took it over completely in 1848. David Carnegie had a partner, W. Robertson. In 1850, when both Nonnen and Robertson had died, ther
2 CONC e was founded a company under Oscar Ekman. Carnegie apparently returned to Scotland in 1841, and four years later married his uncle's daughter. They lived in Scotland but particularly during the 1880's D. Carnegie donated large su
2 CONC ms of money to Gothenburg.
